as  economists predict elevated inflation is  here to stay for the rest of the year  and beyond and gaviola global news  toronto  still ahead the long road to clean  drinking water for a first nation  welcome back for the first time in more  than two decades shoal lake 41st nation  has clean running water as marny bunt  explains it marks the end of one of  canada's longest boil water advisories  a new day and a new sense of hope on  show lake 40 first nation nestled on the  manitoba ontario border there is joy and  relief here in marking the end of a 24  year boil water advisory the emotions  running through today is like  it's unbelievable  also  it's about that time the community  opening a new water treatment plant and  finally turning on the taps  clean drinking water for the first time  in decades despite living on the lake  that is the source of winnipeg's water  supply we struggled  for over two years with bottled water  deliveries  and  the reality of the project coming to an  end is  surreal a milestone for a community also  opening a new school this week all made  possible following the opening of  freedom road two years ago a 14  kilometer gravel stretch connecting the  once cut off community to the  trans-canada highway before then travel  in and out came only by barge or ice  road it's heartwarming actually to even  to be  to be a witness here and being a part of  it and this is where i'm from we finally  have that  water where we don't have to boil it  in order to drink it shoal lake is one  of 105 first nations communities the  federal government has pledged to lift  long-term boil water advisories on a  promise that's been a long time coming  and want to acknowledge that this should  never  have happened in the first place  there is no excuse  that the federal government can present  for any first nation  being on a long-term water advisory in a  country such as ours  now schull lake has a new sense of hope  and a new beginning i'm just  speechless  and a clear path to a better future  marnie blunt global news show lake on  the manitoba ontario border  back in 2015 the federal liberals  promised to end all boil water  advisories on first nations by march of  this year many have been lifted but 51  communities still do not have clean  drinking water on monday liberal party  leader justin trudeau was asked about  his party's efforts towards indigenous  reconciliation during an interview with  global bc's neither garcia here's a bit  of that exchange  i did speak to tacoma schweppe mc chief  roseanne kasmir this weekend and she  calls your government pace of change  meaningfully when it comes to  reconciliation glacial and the amount  that you have prioritized reconciliation  so far in this campaign contrary to what  you just said  completely disappointing what do you say  to her and other indigenous people  across this country that feel the same  way i agree with the impatience that  people feel i agree with the impatience  that indigenous people feel because i  feel it too we need to be moving as fast  as possible but it's not just for the  federal government to decide we're going  to do this we're going to do that we  have to work with the communities  next one candidate's unique path to  politics  thousands of canadians are putting their  names on the ballot for monday's  election including dozens of first-time  candidates each with their own unique  reasons for their political ambitions  mike armstrong has the story of one  candidate's journey from secret service  to public servant  it's exciting there is something  completely different about running for  office and what huda mukbil used to do  for a living  campaigning means putting your name and  face all over that was something she  absolutely didn't do working as an  intelligence officer with canada's spy  agency i was in the security and  intelligence world uh in the shadows for  so many years  hi  i'm running for the next federal  election campaigning is about as public  as it gets mukbil's old job was about as  private as it gets  she spent 15 years at csis the canadian  security intelligence service i was not  in the public eye at all were you even  able to tell people you worked for csis  no my immediate family knew  but  my friends and  members of the community and  yeah  no one knew  my family fled civil war in ethiopia  mark bill's life is now a pretty open  book as a first-time candidate running  for the ndp the main issues that pushed  her into politics she says are systemic  racism and a lack of representation the  same issues that pushed her out of ces  it was hard you know what i went through  was really hard and buckville was part  of a lawsuit along with four other  employees against ceases they accused  the organization of harassment and  discrimination it was eventually settled  out of court and the csis director has  admitted the agency's culture needs to  evolve this year they're hiring the most  diverse class they ever have at thesis  so  so my work there is done  i have had the opportunity mukbil is now  hoping to take those issues to  parliament but to get there she'll need  an upset she's running against a liberal  incumbent who's won six elections in a  row it won't be easy and it's a big  change yeah she's literally gone from no  one knowing the job she had to everyone  knowing the job she wants  mike armstrong global news ottawa  and donna friesen will anchor our  extensive coverage on election night  monday right here on global television  as well as on history global news radio  and various online platforms including  globalnews.ca  and tomorrow donna will broadcast from  the last stop on our road trip ahead of  the election from brampton ontario  that's global national for this  wednesday i'm jeff semple thanks for  watching have a great night  [Music]  [Applause]  you
 

